WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.467
-How is the fam, by the way?
-The fam is good.

00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:04.501
It's summer.
They're just chilling out.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:08.000
-I got to see them randomly on
the street with your husband,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.567
and I saw them.
They're very cute.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:10.767
How old are they now?

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:12.300
-They're 11. They're twins.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.501
-Are they 11?
-Yeah.

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:15.868
-Wow.
-We're spending time.

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:17.834
It's a cool age, 11.
-It's happening fast.

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:19.734
-Your girls are how old?
-8 and 7.

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:21.667
-Yeah, so it's like -- it's
a fun age, where you don't

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:23.501
have to really micromanage them
all of the time.

00:00:23.501 --> 00:00:25.934
And you can sort of hang out
and coexist with them, right?

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:27.200
[ Laughter ]
-Correct.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.033
-Ish?
-That's exactly right.

00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:32.167
Yeah, I can coexist.
-They're watching content.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.033
Gideon is a big gamer.
He loves "Minecraft."

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.868
-Roblox?
-He likes the Roblox alright.

00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:40.734
Harper is into horror movies.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.300
-See, that's --
Isn't it interesting?

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.734
They're twins.
-Yeah.

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:45.267
-You feed them the same food.
-Yeah.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:47.601
-You read them the same book.
-Raise them gender neutral.

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:49.567
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly, and here they are.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:51.934
Two different human beings.
-Totally different.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:54.634
What do you say to a girl who
wants to watch horror movies?

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:56.367
Like, do you say
"absolutely not"?

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.067
Because then she wants to
watch them more.

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.567
She's just seen "It" now. Scary.
-No.

00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:02.067
I can't even watch the trailer
for "It."

00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:03.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.167 --> 00:01:05.601
-She binge-watched
"Stranger Things."

00:01:05.601 --> 00:01:08.400
So, she's, like -- not
horror-horror, but still scary.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.133
-It's up there.
-And so she keeps asking me,

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:13.033
"Papa, what is the scariest
movie that you've ever seen?"

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.234
And I know where this is going
'cause I -- I said,

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:17.400
"I'm not going to tell you
the scariest movie"

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.434
'cause she's going to
want to watch that."

00:01:18.434 --> 00:01:19.701
-Of course.
-"Papa, what's the --

00:01:19.701 --> 00:01:21.400
Please tell me the scariest
movie you've ever seen."

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.934
I said, "The Shining."
[ Audience murmurs ]

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:25.634
So she says,
"Can I watch 'The Shining?'"

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:26.801
[ Laughter ]
-Of course.

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:28.467
-And I said "No, you can't watch
'The Shining.'

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:31.767
'The Shining' is really
psychologically terrifying."

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.200
-Yeah.
-And so, she just kept asking

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.634
and persisting, and so we
watched "The Shining" last week.

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:39.367
[ Laughter ]
-Wow, what a beautiful family.

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.534
Father-daughter moment.

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:41.968
-I don't know
if it was a good call.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:45.067
But I --
And it is still really scary.

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:46.734
-It is, right?
-Jack Nicholson.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:48.634
Super freaky, bloody elevator.

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:50.400
-Oh, the blood flows
through the hall

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.868
and the twin on the tricycle.
-The girls staring. Scary.

00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:55.834
And she was like -- I said,
"So, what'd you think?"

00:01:55.834 --> 00:01:58.167
She said,
"I'm mildly disappointed."

00:01:58.167 --> 00:02:00.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:04.000
-"I'm mildly disappointed"?
-"I'm a bit disappointed."

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.234
-I love that she said that.
-"Well, why?"

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:09.467
She said, "That was the scariest
movie you've ever seen?"

00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:10.901
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.901 --> 00:02:13.567
I was like, "It's not scary like
2022 scary."

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.734
-Yeah, it's not jump-scare,
yeah.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.434
-And she was like,
"Yeah, it wasn't that scary."

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:19.734
[ Laughter ]
-Wow! Good for her.

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:21.901
-And she has slept in our bed
for like the last four nights.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:23.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:24.801
-Ah, reality, reality.

00:02:24.801 --> 00:02:26.100
It was a little scary,
wasn't it?

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.300
-I think it was a little scarier
than she thought.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.467
-I want to talk to you
about something

00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:29.667
you started about a year ago.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.767
This thing is called
"Wondercade."

00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:34.100
-"Wondercade," yeah.
-And I think it's a great word.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.133
If you go to wondercade.com,
it's free.

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:38.434
It's a newsletter.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:40.400
-Weekly?
-Weekly.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.033
-Weekly newsletter.
-"Wondercade Wednesdays."

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:43.434
Just happened today.
-Yeah.

00:02:43.434 --> 00:02:45.200
Explain to people what
Wondercade is

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.667
and why did you start this?

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.267
I love that you did it.

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:52.834
-I'm old enough now, and I have
a lot of varied weird interests

00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:57.901
of like magic and circus and
traveling and I like mixology.

00:02:57.901 --> 00:03:00.400
My husband is an actor as well
as a chef.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.033
And so we talk a lot of things.

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:04.868
I host Tony awards and say like,
"Come see Broadway shows."

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:07.234
So I feel like I have
some things to talk about

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:09.300
in the entertainment world.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.667
And so I thought it would be fun
to do sort of

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:12.901
a weekly missive
of random things.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:16.167
So I came up with "wonder,"
like "wonder" and "wonderful,"

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:18.200
and "cade,"
like "arcade" or "cavalcade."

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.167
And I put them together,
"Wondercade,"

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:22.968
because the URL was available.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.133
[ Laughter ]
So I own the site.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.801
So you go to wondercade.com,
you put in your e-mail.

00:03:26.801 --> 00:03:28.067
That's it.
That's the whole thing.

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:29.734
-You invented the word
"Wondercade."

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:32.501
-"Wondercade."
-It sounds like a word I know.

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:34.133
-Thank you.
-It's fantastic, well done.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:35.701
-And now more people know it.

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:37.467
And every Wednesday,
it's just stuff.

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.234
Like, I'll teach how to
make a Negroni.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:41.300
The one that just came out was
all about farmers market.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.734
David went with Michael Anthony,
who's an amazing chef.

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:46.634
From Gramercy Tavern. And they
went to a farmers market.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.734
And then they sort of explain
when you go to a farmers market

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.667
what you should look for
and some recipes on that.

00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:53.000
It ends with a puzzle.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.601
It's kind of, like, a fun weekly
thing, you know.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.734
-I love this. "Wondercade,"
everyone check it out.

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:58.367
If you go there,
just type your e-mail in.

00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:00.667
You get a fun e-mail.
You go, "Oh, this is awesome."

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:03.100
I love it.
-Thank you.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.567
Very cool that
you mentioned that.

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:06.467
I appreciate that.
-Oh, please. I'm on.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.300
I'm signed up. I get it.
I love it.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.834
I want to talk about "Uncoupled"

00:04:09.834 --> 00:04:11.701
because I'm very excited
about this.

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:15.133
I love a good show,
and I love -- I do.

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:17.033
And I love who's behind it.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:18.334
Can you tell everyone
who's behind the show?

00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:21.067
-Sure, Darren Star
is the co-creator.

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:22.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
-"Sex and the City."

00:04:22.934 --> 00:04:24.234
-"Sex and the City."
-"Emily in Paris."

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:26.300
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-I mean, come on!

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.300
That's up my alley, man.
I love that.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.000
-They're only half-hour
episodes.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.801
They come out on Fridays.
There's eight of them.

00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:33.634
It's about -- My character,
Michael,

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:36.868
is in a 17-year relationship
with his partner, Colin.

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:39.300
And Michael thinks everything is
going well.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.634
And on his 50th birthday --
not Michael's, but Colin's --

00:04:42.634 --> 00:04:44.100
Michael's throwing him
a surprise party.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.834
And just as he's about to open
the door at this big event space

00:04:46.834 --> 00:04:49.901
with hundreds of people waiting,
Colin says, "I've taken all

00:04:49.901 --> 00:04:52.200
my stuff and I've left
and we're no longer together."

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.701
And then, "Surprise!"
[ Laughter ]

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:56.033
-And then the door opens, "Hi!"
-Big party.

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:59.901
So the whole series is like
a breakup show of what happens

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:04.834
when you've been sort of sucker
punched with terrible news

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.901
and you're single again
in New York City.

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:09.534
So it's a comedy.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:11.400
[ Laughter ]
-There are great jokes in there.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.634
-But it's sort of -- I mean,
it is Darren Star, so it's got

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:17.767
real-estate agents, so it's got
some New York, you know,

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:19.734
real-estate porn kind of vibe.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:21.200
The city is a character
within it,

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.467
which I think
you can appreciate.

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:23.868
It's got a nice feel.
-Real-estate porn.

00:05:23.868 --> 00:05:27.100
It does. If you know Darren Star
or know any of those shows,

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.501
it's just some good acting.

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:32.033
There's some good puzzles
in there, conundrums,

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:34.133
and life lessons,
but it's also fun.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.100
-Some pithy conversation.
-Pithy is a very good word.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.501
-And then you kind of
shed a tear.

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:40.968
I'm really proud of it. It's
tonally an interesting show.

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:43.634
But breakups are -- by design --

00:05:43.634 --> 00:05:45.701
-And it all comes out Friday?
-Yeah, all Friday.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:47.400
-So you can binge-watch
the whole thing?

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.400
-Breakups aren't always sad.
You know?

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.267
There's funny things that happen
in life through sadness.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:57.167
-Yes, through your sadness,
breakups become fun.

00:05:57.167 --> 00:06:00.200
-Right, and I thought making
a gay show on Netflix

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.300
about a gay relationship that's
ending would be very niche.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.434
Right? Only for like gay men of
a certain age.

00:06:05.434 --> 00:06:08.334
And it turns out that breakups
are pretty universal

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:09.567
and pretty relatable.
You know what I mean?

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.234
-Oh, yeah, trust me. Yeah.

00:06:11.234 --> 00:06:13.667
We just had a thing of
why I'm single on our show.

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:15.467
Yeah, everyone can relate to
this all.

00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:16.968
It's very, very good.
Very funny.

00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:18.133
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:20.100
Here's Neil Patrick Harris
in "Uncoupled."

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.300
Take a look at this.
-Isn't it great?

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.300
I don't even care that we're
the oldest gays in the club.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.767
No, honey, you keep dancing.

00:06:26.767 --> 00:06:28.434
We have just as much right
to be here as --

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:30.968
-I think that's Colin.

00:06:30.968 --> 00:06:32.968
-Well, first of all,
you look fantastic.

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:35.467
-You really do. So hot.

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.667
It's not him. Oh, thank God.

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:40.601
-I'm not even sure if I'm happy
or not that that wasn't him.

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:41.968
I need a drink.

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.601
-Mikey, there's only one way

00:06:43.601 --> 00:06:45.000
you're going to move on
from this.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.934
-Yeah, it's been one whole week.

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:48.734
Enough with the wallowing.
-Sorry.

00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:51.067
I just think if you want to
start feeling better,

00:06:51.067 --> 00:06:52.934
you got to pop your Colin cherry
and get laid.

00:06:52.934 --> 00:06:55.767
-So eloquent.
Sadly, I don't disagree.

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:57.934
-So glad to know that you're
both on the same page

00:06:57.934 --> 00:06:59.634
about what's best for me.

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:02.033
Three vodka sodas, please.

00:07:02.033 --> 00:07:03.467
-What do you guys want?

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:05.834
[ Laughter ]
-Very nice.

00:07:05.834 --> 00:07:08.400
"What do you guys want?"
Neil Patrick Harris, everybody.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.901
"Uncoupled" premieres
this Friday on Netflix.

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:13.968
Cara Delevingne joins us
when we come back.

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:14.968
Stick around, everybody.

